chedules / cron"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table stakes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schedule pause/resume/backfill controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Catch up to Temporal, Inngest, Trigger.dev"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🔴"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable queues / concurrency controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Catch up to managed task platforms"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Version routing for paused runs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Catch up to Temporal and Trigger.dev"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run search, replay, retry, and retention operations"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Catch up to mature control planes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Devtools / dashboard included"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Catch up to managed platforms, TanStack-style"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed control plane included"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intentional non-goal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🔴"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"🟡"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"product-shape","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product shape"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product / library"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Form factor"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Programming model"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence boundary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Portability"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best fit"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headless core plus optional capability packages for stores, timers, schedules, queues, and devtools."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createWorkflow"},{"type":"text","value":" + explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.step"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.sleep"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.waitForEvent"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.approve"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WorkflowStore"},{"type":"text","value":" you own or configure. Store adapters provide event logs, run state, timer indexes, schedule records, leases, search indexes, and retention."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"High. Same workflow code across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel, and custom infrastructure."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Teams that want TanStack-style primitives, app-embedded durable execution, framework portability, and control over persistence."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Workflows"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare-managed workflow product."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Worker workflow classes, steps, sleeps, events."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare managed workflow state."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Low to medium. Cloudflare-oriented."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare-native durable workflows."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Temporal"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dedicated workflow server/control plane plus workers."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deterministic workflow code + Activities, Signals, Queries, Updates."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Temporal event history in Temporal service/database."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Medium. Portable infrastructure, but you operate or adopt Temporal."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Teams that want the deepest mature workflow control plane."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inngest"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed/serverless event workflow platform."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event-triggered functions with durable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"step.*"},{"type":"text","value":" APIs."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inngest managed history/state."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Medium. Framework integrations, but platform-bound."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serverless event workflows with strong managed DX."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trigger.dev"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed/self-hostable background task platform."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tasks, waits, queues, schedules."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trigger managed/self-hosted task run state."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Medium. Self-hostable, but task-platform oriented."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Background jobs, queues, schedules, replays, and operational task UX."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AWS Step Functions"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed state machine service."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"JSON/YAML or SDK-defined state machines."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AWS managed execution history."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Low to medium. AWS-native."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enterprise/serverless workflows inside AWS."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Workflow / WDK"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed Vercel platform plus open SDK/world abstraction."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"use workflow\""},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"use step\""},{"type":"text","value":" directives and WDK APIs."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel managed persistence for state/event logs on Vercel; swappable worlds in WDK."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Medium. WDK has worlds, but Vercel Workflow value is Vercel-managed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best native Vercel choice when you want Vercel to own the workflow platform."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"adjacent-systems","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adjacent systems"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These products are still useful reference points, but they are narrower,\nnewer, or more architectural than the main comparison set."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product / library"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why it matters"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why it is not in the main matrix"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Netlify Async Workloads"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Important partner deployment-native reference for Netlify and useful for side-by-side demos with TanStack Workflow on Netlify."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is Netlify-specific and less widely evaluated as a standalone workflow platform."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DBOS"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strong reference for Postgres-backed durable execution, schedules, and queues."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is more of a database-backed application/runtime architecture than a broad workflow-platform comparison target."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Restate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strong reference for durable service handlers, virtual objects, workflow routing, and deployment versioning."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is adjacent to workflows, but the main mental model is durable services rather than common app-embedded workflow primitives."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-to-read-this","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How to read this"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow is intentionally smaller than managed workflow platforms. The\ncore engine handles deterministic replay, durable steps, pauses, signals,\napprovals, and version routing. Capability packages provide production store,\ntimer, schedule, queue, host, and devtools integrations without making the core\nengine own your deployment target, database, cron system, queue, or dashboard."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That split is the point:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want a managed platform with an included control plane, Cloudflare\nWorkflows, Temporal, Inngest, Trigger.dev, AWS Step Functions, or Vercel\nWorkflow may be the right fit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want a TypeScript workflow engine that can run inside your app, on your\nchosen host, against your chosen durable store, TanStack Workflow is the\nheadless option."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The production surface is assembled from capability packages: durable store\nadapters, timer drivers, schedule records, queue/concurrency helpers, host\nadapters, and devtools."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The advantage is not that TanStack Workflow has a bigger control plane. The\nadvantage is that it does not need one to be useful. It gives you durable\nexecution as a composable app primitive, then lets your infrastructure remain\nyour infrastructure."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vercel-workflow-note","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Workflow note"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Workflow is an important comparison for serverless TypeScript apps. It\nhas a similar developer promise: write normal async TypeScript, split durable\nwork into steps, sleep without consuming compute, resume from external events,\nand let the platform handle persistence and replay."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The difference is the product boundary."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Workflow / WDK"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What do you adopt?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A managed Vercel workflow platform, powered by WDK, Vercel Functions, Vercel Queues, and Vercel-managed persistence."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A headless TypeScript engine inside your app, plus whichever store/timer/schedule adapters you choose."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do you mark durable code?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Directive model: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"use workflow\""},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"use step\""},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit API model: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createWorkflow"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.step"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.sleep"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.waitForEvent"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.approve"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where does persistence live?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On Vercel, state and event logs live in Vercel-managed persistence. WDK also has swappable \"worlds\" such as Postgres for self-hosting."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" you own or configure. The event log, run state, timer index, and schedule records are part of the adapter contract."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Who owns timers?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Workflow owns the timer system. Long sleeps likely combine persisted deadlines with bounded queue wakeups."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core records the deadline. A timer driver/store adapter claims due timers and resumes runs."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Who owns operations UI?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel dashboard and Workflow observability."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack devtools/admin APIs are planned capability packages."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best fit"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Teams already deploying on Vercel that want the managed workflow product and do not mind the Vercel runtime boundary."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Teams that want durable workflows to stay part of their app architecture and remain portable across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel, and custom infrastructure."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So the positioning is not \"TanStack Workflow works on Vercel and Vercel\nWorkflow doesn't.\" Vercel owns the Vercel-native experience."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The positioning is:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use Vercel Workflow when you want Vercel to be the workflow platform. Use\nTanStack Workflow when you want durable execution as an app-embedded, explicit,\nstorage-adapter-driven TanStack primitive."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"current-recommendation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Current recommendation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For production-style TanStack Workflow deployments, use a durable store as the\nsystem boundary. Postgres is the best first target because it can hold run state,\nthe append-only event log, due-timer indexes, schedule records, leases, run\nsearch indexes, and retention policies in one portable substrate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On serverless hosts like Cloudflare, Netlify, and Vercel, provider cron or\nscheduled functions should wake bounded timer sweeps. The database decides\nwhether a workflow is actually due to resume."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"capability-matrix","text":"Capability matrix","level":2},{"id":"product-shape","text":"Product shape","level":2},{"id":"adjacent-systems","text":"Adjacent systems","level":2},{"id":"how-to-read-this","text":"How to read this","level":2},{"id":"vercel-workflow-note","text":"Vercel Workflow note","level":2},{"id":"current-recommendation","text":"Current recommendation","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nChoosing a durable workflow engine is mostly a question of where you want the\ndurability boundary to live. Some products give you a managed control plane.\nTanStack Workflow gives you a headless TypeScript engine and lets you choose the\nruntime, store, scheduler, and deployment target.\n\nThat is the core positioning: durable execution for TypeScript apps without\nrequiring your application logic to move into a workflow platform. Use a managed\nworkflow product when you want that product to own execution. Use TanStack\nWorkflow when you want workflows to remain app-embedded, storage-adapter-driven,\nand portable across hosts.\n\nThis comparison shows how TanStack Workflow fits against the workflow systems\nteams commonly evaluate. It focuses on product shape and operational trade-offs,\nnot every feature or pricing detail. If you use one of these systems and see\nsomething that should be corrected, please suggest a change with notes or source\nlinks.\n\n## Capability matrix\n\nLegend: ✅ first-class fit, 🟡 possible or partial fit, 🔴 not a primary fit.\nThe TanStack signal column explains whether the row is a differentiator, table\nstakes, or an area where competitors set the bar we need to meet.\nThis matrix compares product fit, not identical APIs. When public documentation\ndoes not clearly support a first-class claim, the cell is marked partial.\n\n| Capability | TanStack signal | TanStack Workflow | Cloudflare Workflows | Temporal | Inngest | Trigger.dev | AWS Step Functions | Vercel Workflow / WDK |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Headless library embedded in your app | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🟡 |\n| Bring your own durable store | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🟡 |\n| No required external service | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🔴 |\n| Runs across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🟡 | ✅ | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🟡 |\n| Plain TypeScript workflow authoring | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ |\n| Explicit durable steps | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Durable sleep / timers | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Wait for external events | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Human approval gates | Catch up to Temporal-style workflows | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | 🟡 | 🟡 | 🟡 | 🟡 |\n| Recurring schedules / cron | Table stakes | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Schedule pause/resume/backfill controls | Catch up to Temporal, Inngest, Trigger.dev | 🟡 | 🔴 | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 | 🟡 | 🟡 |\n| Durable queues / concurrency controls | Catch up to managed task platforms | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Version routing for paused runs | Catch up to Temporal and Trigger.dev | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 |\n| Run search, replay, retry, and retention operations | Catch up to mature control planes | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 |\n| Devtools / dashboard included | Catch up to managed platforms, TanStack-style | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Managed control plane included | Intentional non-goal | 🔴 | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n\n## Product shape\n\n| Product / library | Form factor | Programming model | Persistence boundary | Portability | Best fit |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **TanStack Workflow** | Headless core plus optional capability packages for stores, timers, schedules, queues, and devtools. | `createWorkflow` + explicit `ctx.step`, `ctx.sleep`, `ctx.waitForEvent`, `ctx.approve`. | A `WorkflowStore` you own or configure. Store adapters provide event logs, run state, timer indexes, schedule records, leases, search indexes, and retention. | High. Same workflow code across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel, and custom infrastructure. | Teams that want TanStack-style primitives, app-embedded durable execution, framework portability, and control over persistence. |\n| **Cloudflare Workflows** | Cloudflare-managed workflow product. | Worker workflow classes, steps, sleeps, events. | Cloudflare managed workflow state. | Low to medium. Cloudflare-oriented. | Cloudflare-native durable workflows. |\n| **Temporal** | Dedicated workflow server/control plane plus workers. | Deterministic workflow code + Activities, Signals, Queries, Updates. | Temporal event history in Temporal service/database. | Medium. Portable infrastructure, but you operate or adopt Temporal. | Teams that want the deepest mature workflow control plane. |\n| **Inngest** | Managed/serverless event workflow platform. | Event-triggered functions with durable `step.*` APIs. | Inngest managed history/state. | Medium. Framework integrations, but platform-bound. | Serverless event workflows with strong managed DX. |\n| **Trigger.dev** | Managed/self-hostable background task platform. | Tasks, waits, queues, schedules. | Trigger managed/self-hosted task run state. | Medium. Self-hostable, but task-platform oriented. | Background jobs, queues, schedules, replays, and operational task UX. |\n| **AWS Step Functions** | Managed state machine service. | JSON/YAML or SDK-defined state machines. | AWS managed execution history. | Low to medium. AWS-native. | Enterprise/serverless workflows inside AWS. |\n| **Vercel Workflow / WDK** | Managed Vercel platform plus open SDK/world abstraction. | `\"use workflow\"` / `\"use step\"` directives and WDK APIs. | Vercel managed persistence for state/event logs on Vercel; swappable worlds in WDK. | Medium. WDK has worlds, but Vercel Workflow value is Vercel-managed. | Best native Vercel choice when you want Vercel to own the workflow platform. |\n\n## Adjacent systems\n\nThese products are still useful reference points, but they are narrower,\nnewer, or more architectural than the main comparison set.\n\n| Product / library | Why it matters | Why it is not in the main matrix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Netlify Async Workloads** | Important partner deployment-native reference for Netlify and useful for side-by-side demos with TanStack Workflow on Netlify. | It is Netlify-specific and less widely evaluated as a standalone workflow platform. |\n| **DBOS** | Strong reference for Postgres-backed durable execution, schedules, and queues. | It is more of a database-backed application/runtime architecture than a broad workflow-platform comparison target. |\n| **Restate** | Strong reference for durable service handlers, virtual objects, workflow routing, and deployment versioning. | It is adjacent to workflows, but the main mental model is durable services rather than common app-embedded workflow primitives. |\n\n## How to read this\n\nTanStack Workflow is intentionally smaller than managed workflow platforms. The\ncore engine handles deterministic replay, durable steps, pauses, signals,\napprovals, and version routing. Capability packages provide production store,\ntimer, schedule, queue, host, and devtools integrations without making the core\nengine own your deployment target, database, cron system, queue, or dashboard.\n\nThat split is the point:\n\n- If you want a managed platform with an included control plane, Cloudflare\n  Workflows, Temporal, Inngest, Trigger.dev, AWS Step Functions, or Vercel\n  Workflow may be the right fit.\n- If you want a TypeScript workflow engine that can run inside your app, on your\n  chosen host, against your chosen durable store, TanStack Workflow is the\n  headless option.\n- The production surface is assembled from capability packages: durable store\n  adapters, timer drivers, schedule records, queue/concurrency helpers, host\n  adapters, and devtools.\n\nThe advantage is not that TanStack Workflow has a bigger control plane. The\nadvantage is that it does not need one to be useful. It gives you durable\nexecution as a composable app primitive, then lets your infrastructure remain\nyour infrastructure.\n\n## Vercel Workflow note\n\nVercel Workflow is an important comparison for serverless TypeScript apps. It\nhas a similar developer promise: write normal async TypeScript, split durable\nwork into steps, sleep without consuming compute, resume from external events,\nand let the platform handle persistence and replay.\n\nThe difference is the product boundary.\n\n| Question | Vercel Workflow / WDK | TanStack Workflow |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What do you adopt? | A managed Vercel workflow platform, powered by WDK, Vercel Functions, Vercel Queues, and Vercel-managed persistence. | A headless TypeScript engine inside your app, plus whichever store/timer/schedule adapters you choose. |\n| How do you mark durable code? | Directive model: `\"use workflow\"` and `\"use step\"`. | Explicit API model: `createWorkflow`, `ctx.step`, `ctx.sleep`, `ctx.waitForEvent`, `ctx.approve`. |\n| Where does persistence live? | On Vercel, state and event logs live in Vercel-managed persistence. WDK also has swappable \"worlds\" such as Postgres for self-hosting. | In a `RunStore` you own or configure. The event log, run state, timer index, and schedule records are part of the adapter contract. |\n| Who owns timers? | Vercel Workflow owns the timer system. Long sleeps likely combine persisted deadlines with bounded queue wakeups. | Core records the deadline. A timer driver/store adapter claims due timers and resumes runs. |\n| Who owns operations UI? | Vercel dashboard and Workflow observability. | TanStack devtools/admin APIs are planned capability packages. |\n| Best fit | Teams already deploying on Vercel that want the managed workflow product and do not mind the Vercel runtime boundary. | Teams that want durable workflows to stay part of their app architecture and remain portable across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel, and custom infrastructure. |\n\nSo the positioning is not \"TanStack Workflow works on Vercel and Vercel\nWorkflow doesn't.\" Vercel owns the Vercel-native experience.\n\nThe positioning is:\n\n> Use Vercel Workflow when you want Vercel to be the workflow platform. Use\n> TanStack Workflow when you want durable execution as an app-embedded, explicit,\n> storage-adapter-driven TanStack primitive.\n\n## Current recommendation\n\nFor production-style TanStack Workflow deployments, use a durable store as the\nsystem boundary. Postgres is the best first target because it can hold run state,\nthe append-only event log, due-timer indexes, schedule records, leases, run\nsearch indexes, and retention policies in one portable substrate.\n\nOn serverless hosts like Cloudflare, Netlify, and Vercel, provider cron or\nscheduled functions should wake bounded timer sweeps. The database decides\nwhether a workflow is actually due to resume.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: comparison\ntitle: Comparison\ntoc: false\n---\n\nChoosing a durable workflow engine is mostly a question of where you want the\ndurability boundary to live. Some products give you a managed control plane.\nTanStack Workflow gives you a headless TypeScript engine and lets you choose the\nruntime, store, scheduler, and deployment target.\n\nThat is the core positioning: durable execution for TypeScript apps without\nrequiring your application logic to move into a workflow platform. Use a managed\nworkflow product when you want that product to own execution. Use TanStack\nWorkflow when you want workflows to remain app-embedded, storage-adapter-driven,\nand portable across hosts.\n\nThis comparison shows how TanStack Workflow fits against the workflow systems\nteams commonly evaluate. It focuses on product shape and operational trade-offs,\nnot every feature or pricing detail. If you use one of these systems and see\nsomething that should be corrected, please suggest a change with notes or source\nlinks.\n\n## Capability matrix\n\nLegend: ✅ first-class fit, 🟡 possible or partial fit, 🔴 not a primary fit.\nThe TanStack signal column explains whether the row is a differentiator, table\nstakes, or an area where competitors set the bar we need to meet.\nThis matrix compares product fit, not identical APIs. When public documentation\ndoes not clearly support a first-class claim, the cell is marked partial.\n\n| Capability | TanStack signal | TanStack Workflow | Cloudflare Workflows | Temporal | Inngest | Trigger.dev | AWS Step Functions | Vercel Workflow / WDK |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Headless library embedded in your app | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🟡 |\n| Bring your own durable store | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🟡 |\n| No required external service | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🔴 | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🔴 |\n| Runs across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel | Differentiator | ✅ | 🔴 | 🟡 | ✅ | 🟡 | 🔴 | 🟡 |\n| Plain TypeScript workflow authoring | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ |\n| Explicit durable steps | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Durable sleep / timers | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Wait for external events | Table stakes | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Human approval gates | Catch up to Temporal-style workflows | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | 🟡 | 🟡 | 🟡 | 🟡 |\n| Recurring schedules / cron | Table stakes | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Schedule pause/resume/backfill controls | Catch up to Temporal, Inngest, Trigger.dev | 🟡 | 🔴 | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 | 🟡 | 🟡 |\n| Durable queues / concurrency controls | Catch up to managed task platforms | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Version routing for paused runs | Catch up to Temporal and Trigger.dev | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 |\n| Run search, replay, retry, and retention operations | Catch up to mature control planes | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | 🟡 |\n| Devtools / dashboard included | Catch up to managed platforms, TanStack-style | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Managed control plane included | Intentional non-goal | 🔴 | ✅ | 🟡 | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n\n## Product shape\n\n| Product / library | Form factor | Programming model | Persistence boundary | Portability | Best fit |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **TanStack Workflow** | Headless core plus optional capability packages for stores, timers, schedules, queues, and devtools. | `createWorkflow` + explicit `ctx.step`, `ctx.sleep`, `ctx.waitForEvent`, `ctx.approve`. | A `WorkflowStore` you own or configure. Store adapters provide event logs, run state, timer indexes, schedule records, leases, search indexes, and retention. | High. Same workflow code across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel, and custom infrastructure. | Teams that want TanStack-style primitives, app-embedded durable execution, framework portability, and control over persistence. |\n| **Cloudflare Workflows** | Cloudflare-managed workflow product. | Worker workflow classes, steps, sleeps, events. | Cloudflare managed workflow state. | Low to medium. Cloudflare-oriented. | Cloudflare-native durable workflows. |\n| **Temporal** | Dedicated workflow server/control plane plus workers. | Deterministic workflow code + Activities, Signals, Queries, Updates. | Temporal event history in Temporal service/database. | Medium. Portable infrastructure, but you operate or adopt Temporal. | Teams that want the deepest mature workflow control plane. |\n| **Inngest** | Managed/serverless event workflow platform. | Event-triggered functions with durable `step.*` APIs. | Inngest managed history/state. | Medium. Framework integrations, but platform-bound. | Serverless event workflows with strong managed DX. |\n| **Trigger.dev** | Managed/self-hostable background task platform. | Tasks, waits, queues, schedules. | Trigger managed/self-hosted task run state. | Medium. Self-hostable, but task-platform oriented. | Background jobs, queues, schedules, replays, and operational task UX. |\n| **AWS Step Functions** | Managed state machine service. | JSON/YAML or SDK-defined state machines. | AWS managed execution history. | Low to medium. AWS-native. | Enterprise/serverless workflows inside AWS. |\n| **Vercel Workflow / WDK** | Managed Vercel platform plus open SDK/world abstraction. | `\"use workflow\"` / `\"use step\"` directives and WDK APIs. | Vercel managed persistence for state/event logs on Vercel; swappable worlds in WDK. | Medium. WDK has worlds, but Vercel Workflow value is Vercel-managed. | Best native Vercel choice when you want Vercel to own the workflow platform. |\n\n## Adjacent systems\n\nThese products are still useful reference points, but they are narrower,\nnewer, or more architectural than the main comparison set.\n\n| Product / library | Why it matters | Why it is not in the main matrix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Netlify Async Workloads** | Important partner deployment-native reference for Netlify and useful for side-by-side demos with TanStack Workflow on Netlify. | It is Netlify-specific and less widely evaluated as a standalone workflow platform. |\n| **DBOS** | Strong reference for Postgres-backed durable execution, schedules, and queues. | It is more of a database-backed application/runtime architecture than a broad workflow-platform comparison target. |\n| **Restate** | Strong reference for durable service handlers, virtual objects, workflow routing, and deployment versioning. | It is adjacent to workflows, but the main mental model is durable services rather than common app-embedded workflow primitives. |\n\n## How to read this\n\nTanStack Workflow is intentionally smaller than managed workflow platforms. The\ncore engine handles deterministic replay, durable steps, pauses, signals,\napprovals, and version routing. Capability packages provide production store,\ntimer, schedule, queue, host, and devtools integrations without making the core\nengine own your deployment target, database, cron system, queue, or dashboard.\n\nThat split is the point:\n\n- If you want a managed platform with an included control plane, Cloudflare\n  Workflows, Temporal, Inngest, Trigger.dev, AWS Step Functions, or Vercel\n  Workflow may be the right fit.\n- If you want a TypeScript workflow engine that can run inside your app, on your\n  chosen host, against your chosen durable store, TanStack Workflow is the\n  headless option.\n- The production surface is assembled from capability packages: durable store\n  adapters, timer drivers, schedule records, queue/concurrency helpers, host\n  adapters, and devtools.\n\nThe advantage is not that TanStack Workflow has a bigger control plane. The\nadvantage is that it does not need one to be useful. It gives you durable\nexecution as a composable app primitive, then lets your infrastructure remain\nyour infrastructure.\n\n## Vercel Workflow note\n\nVercel Workflow is an important comparison for serverless TypeScript apps. It\nhas a similar developer promise: write normal async TypeScript, split durable\nwork into steps, sleep without consuming compute, resume from external events,\nand let the platform handle persistence and replay.\n\nThe difference is the product boundary.\n\n| Question | Vercel Workflow / WDK | TanStack Workflow |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What do you adopt? | A managed Vercel workflow platform, powered by WDK, Vercel Functions, Vercel Queues, and Vercel-managed persistence. | A headless TypeScript engine inside your app, plus whichever store/timer/schedule adapters you choose. |\n| How do you mark durable code? | Directive model: `\"use workflow\"` and `\"use step\"`. | Explicit API model: `createWorkflow`, `ctx.step`, `ctx.sleep`, `ctx.waitForEvent`, `ctx.approve`. |\n| Where does persistence live? | On Vercel, state and event logs live in Vercel-managed persistence. WDK also has swappable \"worlds\" such as Postgres for self-hosting. | In a `RunStore` you own or configure. The event log, run state, timer index, and schedule records are part of the adapter contract. |\n| Who owns timers? | Vercel Workflow owns the timer system. Long sleeps likely combine persisted deadlines with bounded queue wakeups. | Core records the deadline. A timer driver/store adapter claims due timers and resumes runs. |\n| Who owns operations UI? | Vercel dashboard and Workflow observability. | TanStack devtools/admin APIs are planned capability packages. |\n| Best fit | Teams already deploying on Vercel that want the managed workflow product and do not mind the Vercel runtime boundary. | Teams that want durable workflows to stay part of their app architecture and remain portable across Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, AWS, Vercel, and custom infrastructure. |\n\nSo the positioning is not \"TanStack Workflow works on Vercel and Vercel\nWorkflow doesn't.\" Vercel owns the Vercel-native experience.\n\nThe positioning is:\n\n> Use Vercel Workflow when you want Vercel to be the workflow platform. Use\n> TanStack Workflow when you want durable execution as an app-embedded, explicit,\n> storage-adapter-driven TanStack primitive.\n\n## Current recommendation\n\nFor production-style TanStack Workflow deployments, use a durable store as the\nsystem boundary. Postgres is the best first target because it can hold run state,\nthe append-only event log, due-timer indexes, schedule records, leases, run\nsearch indexes, and retention policies in one portable substrate.\n\nOn serverless hosts like Cloudflare, Netlify, and Vercel, provider cron or\nscheduled functions should wake bounded timer sweeps. The database decides\nwhether a workflow is actually due to resume.\n"}
